   "text": "The pattern indexes to an F-centred pseudo-cubic double perovskite with a \u2248 7.92 \u00c5. Only mp-1218539 (SrCaNbCrO6) matches that cell; I found no peaks needing a second phase.\n\nObserved peaks and their indexing on the \u2248 7.92 \u00c5 cell:\n\n| 2\u03b8 (\u00b0) | Relative intensity | Assignment |\n|---|---|---|\n| 19.4 | ~18 | (111), from B-site ordering |\n| 22.45 | ~11 | (200) |\n| 31.95 | 100 | (220) |\n| 37.7 | ~12 | (311) |\n| 45.8 | ~44 | (400) |\n| 53.9 / 54.6 | ~17 / ~28 | split doublet |\n| 63.5 | ~16 | \u2014 |\n| 71.6 | ~11 | \u2014 |\n\n**Ruled out by lattice size:**\n- The other three rock-salt-ordered double perovskites have cubic cells that are too large. Ba2CaMoO6 is about 8.50 \u00c5, and CaEu2WO6 and Ba2MnWO6 are about 8.35 \u00c5. These would put (220) near 29.7\u00b0 and 30.3\u00b0, not 31.95\u00b0.\n- SrZrO3 and NaLa2TaO6 have a perovskite subcell of about 4.1\u20134.15 \u00c5, again too large for the observed (220) position.\n- Cs(WO3)2 is a pyrochlore with a \u2248 10.5 \u00c5, far too large.\n- Li2Mn3FeO8 is a spinel with a cubic-equivalent cell of about 8.4 \u00c5.\n- Rb3ScV2O8 is trigonal and would not give this simple cubic-type peak sequence.\n\n**Match:** SrCaNbCrO6 has a = 5.58, b = 7.91, c = 5.63 \u00c5. Its perovskite subcell is about 3.96 \u00c5, so the doubled cell is \u2248 7.92 \u00c5, as observed. The Nb/Cr ordering explains the (111) superlattice peak, and the slight distortion from cubic explains the split doublet near 54\u00b0.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1218539\"], \"confidence\": 0.85, \"note\": \"Pattern indexes to an F-centred pseudo-cubic double perovskite with a\u22487.92 \u00c5, matching only SrCaNbCrO6 (a_p\u22483.96 \u00c5); other candidates have the wrong lattice size and no unexplained peaks remain.\"}",
